# MedOS encrypted data volume.
#
# Intentionally empty. The med-data volume is owned by
# med-data-provision.service, not by systemd-cryptsetup, for two reasons that
# only became visible once the A/B disk layout was real:
#
#  1. wic cannot write a LUKS header, so med-data arrives as plain ext4 and has
#     to be converted on first boot. A crypttab entry makes systemd try to open
#     it before anything has had the chance to create it, and the unit fails on
#     every boot of an unprovisioned device.
#
#  2. The development key lives on the ESP, because the rootfs is an A/B slot
#     and a key stored there would be destroyed by the first successful update.
#     systemd-cryptsetup runs before local-fs.target, so it cannot read a key
#     from a filesystem that is not mounted yet, and ordering the ESP mount
#     earlier would be a dependency cycle. The provisioning unit mounts the ESP
#     privately instead.
#
# The tpm2 key source will not have either problem - the key is unsealed by the
# TPM rather than read from a filesystem - so it is expected to come back here
# as a real entry:
#
#   med-data  /dev/disk/by-partlabel/med-data  none  luks,discard,tpm2-device=auto
#
# See docs/implementation_plan_luks.md.
